# Cloudflare Pages response headers.
#
# The problem this solves: without an explicit cache policy, a browser can keep
# a stale index.html after a new deploy. That old HTML references the previous
# build's content-hashed bundle (e.g. index-ABC123.js), which no longer exists
# once a new deploy lands — so Cloudflare serves the SPA fallback (index.html,
# text/html) for that .js request. The browser rejects it ("Expected a
# JavaScript module … responded with MIME type text/html") and the page renders
# blank until a hard refresh.
#
# Fix: the HTML entry (and every SPA-fallback route, which also serves
# index.html) must revalidate on each load so a new deploy is picked up
# immediately. Vite's content-hashed assets under /assets/ are immutable — their
# filename changes every build — so they're safe to cache forever. The more
# specific /assets/* rule is declared last so it wins for asset paths.

/*
  Cache-Control: no-cache

/assets/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable
